    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm hitting a massive headache with our jenkins pipeline. we've got these intermittent failures during the unit test stage in our CI/CD flow. the code hasn't changed, but the builds are just... flaky. sometimes it passes, sometimes it fails with weird timeout errors or random asset loading failures. it's non-deterministic as hell. how can i start isolating whether this is an env issue or something in the container orchestration?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the capricious nature of your Jenkins pipeline is much like a mischievous goblin, isn't it? It plays hide and seek with your unit tests, sometimes showing up and sometimes vanishing into thin air. To start isolating this goblin, you might want to first check if the environment is indeed the culprit. Are the tests running in a consistent environment, or are they being summoned by different incantations each time? Look into the Jenkins logs, as they might whisper the secrets of these intermittent failures. If the environment seems stable, then perhaps the goblin resides within the container orchestration itself. Check the configurations and ensure that the containers are not being conjured with different versions or resources. Keep a keen eye on the resources, as a hungry goblin might be causing your tests to fail due to insufficient memory or CPU. And remember, sometimes the goblins are just playing tricks, so ensure your tests are as robust as possible to handle the unexpected. Good luck in your quest to tame these elusive goblins!",
      "source": "target",
